  "what_happened_next": {
    "text": "Disney led the set, but the two homebuilders split sharply: Lennar gained while KB Home lost nearly three-fifths. Bonds gained more than the US basket. Housing trouble was already visible at the start, yet it still did not tell you which companies would recover or how long that would take.\n\nLehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy the following September as the financial crisis grew worse. The period also included the Deepwater Horizon disaster and Japan’s earthquake and tsunami. The chart covers years in which people faced several very different kinds of uncertainty.\n\nThe two homebuilders offer a concrete test of an industry story. Someone might have expected housing to recover, yet Lennar and KB Home did not give the same five-year result. Owning both would still have placed a lot of money in one kind of business. A broad basket spreads that reliance more widely, though it can also fall. The useful question is how much your plan depends on one forecast being right.",
    "source_ids": [
      "french-us",
      "french-international",
      "fred-gs5",
      "yahoo-len",
      "yahoo-kbh",
      "yahoo-dis",
      "financial-crisis",
      "deepwater",
      "japan-quake"
    ]
  },
  "reflection": {
    "what_people_were_focused_on": "The housing slowdown was spreading into finance. Branding, education and television also competed for attention.",
    "what_actually_mattered": "Companies in the same industry had different results. Owning two homebuilders was not the same as spreading money across many unrelated businesses.",
    "what_faded_away": "A builder’s sales incentive did not reveal its five-year stock return. A popular entertainment brand did not provide a precise forecast either.",
    "what_nobody_knew": "The depth of the financial crisis and the eventual company rankings were unknown. Knowing there was trouble was different from knowing its outcome.",
